cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use heliofleet-mcp in your research, please cite it as below, together with HelioFleet (and HelioField for coronal-field results)."
title: "heliofleet-mcp: spacecraft geometry and solar magnetic connectivity from HelioFleet and HelioField data"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Dash
    given-names: Soumyaranjan
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0103-6569"
    affiliation: "National Solar Observatory"
version: 0.1.2
date-released: 2026-11-20
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.24409982
url: "https://pypi.org/project/heliofleet-mcp/"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sr-dash/heliofleet-mcp"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  heliofleet-mcp answers questions about heliophysics spacecraft (SDO, SOHO, Aditya-L1, ACE, Wind,
  DSCOVR, IMAP, SOLAR-1, Carruthers, Solar Orbiter, Parker Solar Probe, BepiColombo, STEREO-A/B and
  MAVEN) from HelioFleet's published JPL Horizons ephemerides: positions in Stonyhurst, Carrington,
  ecliptic and HEE frames, viewing geometry (B0, L0, apparent solar radius, light time), and windows
  of radial alignment, quadrature, solar conjunction and magnetic connection. From HelioField's daily
  PFSS products it adds the magnetic connection of each spacecraft to its source region, space-weather
  indicators and coronal-field summaries. It is a Model Context Protocol server, a command line that
  prints JSON, and a Python API. Every result carries a HelioFleet or HelioField link that reproduces
  the view.
keywords:
  - heliophysics
  - spacecraft ephemerides
  - solar physics
  - multi-spacecraft observations
  - magnetic connectivity
  - PFSS
  - space weather
  - JPL Horizons
  - Model Context Protocol
references:
